<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-modeling-diff-eq" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Modeling with Differential Equations</title>
 <objectives>
      <ul>
        <li><p>To understand that a <term>differential equation</term> is an equation relating a function to one or more of its derivatives and that an <term>initial value problem</term> is a differential equation
          <md>\frac{dx}{dt} = f(t,x),</md>
        where the <term>initial condition</term>, <m>x(t_0) = x_0</m>, is specified.</p></li>

        <li><p>To understand and be able to apply the three principal steps in modeling any phenomenon with differential equations.
            <ul>
            
                <li><p>Discovering the differential equation or equations that best describe a specified physical situation.</p></li>
                
                <li><p>Finding<mdash/>either exactly or approximately<mdash/>the appropriate solution of the equation or equations.</p></li>
                
                <li><p>Interpreting the solution in terms of the phenomenon.</p></li>
                
            </ul></p></li>

        <li><p>To understand and be able to apply the differential equations that model <term>exponential growth</term> and <term>logistic growth</term>.</p></li>

        <li><p>To understand that some phenomenon are best modeled by higher order differential equations or systems of differential equations.</p></li>
        
      </ul>

    </objectives>
<introduction>
  <p> If we wish to bring mathematical tools to bear on a problem, we often find that the relationships between the quantities of interest can be expressed as differential equations. 
    A <term>differential equation</term> is an equation that relates a function to one or more of its derivatives. For example, the equation
    <md>\frac{dx}{dt} = 3t^2x</md>
    is a differential equation that relates the function <m>x(t)</m> to its derivative <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m>. If we specify an initial condition, such as <m>x(0) = 2</m>, then we have an initial value problem.
    The solution to this initial value problem is the function <m>x(t) = 2  e^{t^3}</m>, which satisfies both the differential equation and the initial condition. We will see how to find this solution in a later section, but for now we will focus on how to model phenomena with differential equations.
  </p>
</introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-exponential-growth">
    <title>Exponential Growth and Decay</title>
    <p>
      We begin our study of differential equations with a simple example of modeling a phenomenon with a differential equation. Consider the problem of modeling the growth of a population of organisms. 
      Let <m>P(t)</m> be the population at time <m>t</m>. We might hypothesize that the rate of change of the population is proportional to the population itself, which leads us to the differential equation 
      <md>
        \frac{dP}{dt} = k P(t)
      </md>
      This is a first-order linear differential equation, and its solution is given by
      <md>
        P(t) = P_0 e^{kt}
      </md>
      where <m>P_0</m> is the population at time <m>t = 0</m>, the <term>initial population</term>. 
      This model is known as the <term>exponential growth</term> model, and it 
      describes a situation where the population grows without bound (if <m>k\gt 0</m>) or 
      decays to 0 (if <m>k\lt 0</m>) as time 
      increases. However, in reality, populations cannot grow indefinitely due 
      to limited resources, so we will need to modify our model to account for 
      this fact. We will see how to do this in the next section.
    </p>
