</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-What-Is-a-Solution">
  <title>What Is a Solution?</title>
  <p>A <term>solution</term> to a differential equation is a function that 
    satisfies the equation. That is, when we substitute the function and its 
    derivatives into the differential equation, the equation holds true. </p>
  <p>When confronted with a differential equation, we often want to find a 
    solution that satisfies certain initial conditions. For example, if we 
    have the differential equation</p>
    <md>
      \frac{dx}{dt} = 3t^2x
    </md>
    <p>and the initial condition <m>x(0) = 2</m>, we want to find a function <m>x(t)</m> such 
    that when we substitute it into the left-hand side of the equation, we get the same expression as on 
    the right-hand side, and also satisfies the initial condition at <m>t=0</m>.</p>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-Verifying-Solutions">
  <title>Verifying Solutions</title>
  <p>Given a differential equation, the immediate question for most of us is ``How do we solve it?'' 
    We already know from our previous experience in calculus how to solve a differential equation of the form 
    <m>y' = f(x)</m>. The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus tells us that we undo derivatives through <term>antidifferentiation</term>. 
    That is, we take an integral to get that</p>
    <md>y = F(x) + C</md>
    <p>where <m>F</m> is any antiderivative of <m>f</m> and <m>C</m> is an <term>arbitrary constant</term>. That is, <m>C</m> can be any 
    real number.</p>
  <p>The reason, however, why there is an entire course in differential equations is that differential equations don't always have such 
    nice forms. ``Just integrate'' sounds like a great idea, but it can't always be done and when it can, there's often a lot of preparatory 
    work involved in getting us to the point where we <em>can</em> use integration.</p>
  <p>Luckily, if someone <em>proposes</em> a solution, it isn't too difficult to <term>verify</term> the solution. That is, we can quickly 
      check whether the proposed solution makes our differential equation true or not.</p>
</subsection>
</section>